INT. HOSPITAL LAUNDRY ROOM, BASEMENT LEVEL - NIGHT

Steam, lint, the long sigh of industrial dryers. Wire carts of folded scrubs stand in rows like pale choirs. A radio on a shelf murmurs a ball game no one is following.

NURSE DOLORES FAIRWEATHER-NKEMELU (50s, reading glasses on a beaded chain) sits on an overturned bucket, a clipboard on her knees. Across from her, DR. SAMANTHA VOSS-ITO (30s, exhausted, hair half out of its clip) leans against a warm machine with her eyes closed. They have come down here to escape the ward for ten minutes, and neither has said so.

A third woman, LAUNDRY SUPERVISOR BERNADETTE QUILLON (60s, forearms like rope), folds a sheet with the metronomic calm of someone who has done it forty thousand times. Near her, two younger orderlies stack towels: KESTUTIS BALODIS (20s, tall, a shy stammer) and ROSAURA MENDOZA-LIN (20s, a bright scarf around her hair).

DOLORES
Fourteen's stable. She asked for tea.

SAMANTHA
She can have tea.

DOLORES
She wants it at four a.m.

SAMANTHA
Then she wants it at four a.m.

BERNADETTE
(not looking up)
Kettle's by the dryer. Help yourself.

DOLORES
You're an angel, Bernadette.

BERNADETTE
I'm a woman with a kettle.

Samantha opens her eyes. She watches Dolores pour water into a chipped mug, then hand it to Rosaura, who nods and slips away. Dolores sits again; Bernadette snaps a sheet in the air and it floats down in a perfect rectangle.

KESTUTIS
Dr. Voss? M-may I ask?

SAMANTHA
Of course.

KESTUTIS
Does it g-get easier?

Samantha smiles, tired but kind. Dolores glances at Bernadette. Bernadette keeps folding. The dryer ticks down. Nobody answers him for a long moment.

SAMANTHA
It gets quieter.

KESTUTIS
Is that the same?

DOLORES
(gently)
Not really, love.

The radio announcer shouts about a home run. Everyone lets it fill the silence.
